 61: ## 4. Screen and interaction specification
 62: 
 63: All values here are proposed DoloGram implementation targets. Verify against public Messenger references and record adaptations in Phase 0. Existing tokens govern colors and fonts.
 64: 
 65: | State | Structure and action |
 66: |---|---|
 67: | Entry | Retain the existing header search icon and expand-to-field transition. Toolbar is 56 logical pixels plus top inset. Field is 40 pixels high with full radius and a 44-pixel clear-action target. Placeholder is Search. Back is always available. |
 68: | Blank query | Show Recent searches as horizontally scrolling 64-pixel avatars and up to two name lines, with Edit beside the heading. Suggested shows up to ten eligible conversations, newest activity first, using 48-pixel avatars and simple two-line rows. With no history or conversations, show the existing search empty state and an actionable username-lookup entry. |
 69: | Entered query | Show All/People/Groups/Messages pills in a horizontal strip. Preserve the selected category while typing. Clear resets to All. Rows use 16-pixel outer padding, a 12-pixel avatar/text gap, titleSm/bodyMd text, and minimum height 72 pixels that grows for accessibility. |
 70: | All/category results | People rows open a person, Groups open a group, and Messages rows show conversation title plus N matched messages. All limits each section to three rows. See all selects that category and starts it at the top. Empty categories have query-specific feedback. |
 71: | Scoped results | One toolbar field with Back and a visible conversation scope label. Use a virtualized list showing sender, date including year, and up to two snippet lines around the matching text. Continue loading with cursor pagination and retain an accessible Show more action. |
 72: | Message context | Show the original message with surrounding history, readable term highlighting, and the existing focused-row treatment. Restrict search highlighting to the selected message. Jump to latest ends the search highlight treatment. |
 73: | Edit history | Replace the recent-avatar rail with removable rows and Done. Clear all opens the existing confirmation dialog. Cancel preserves history. Storage errors retain existing entries with Retry. Android Back dismisses the dialog, then editing, then the search route according to native keyboard handling. |
 74: | Loading/error | Names can render while messages are counting. Display Counting messages or Searching messages while incomplete. Show exact counts only after completion. A timeout/read error keeps eligible name results usable and supplies Retry for the failed portion. |
 75: 
 76: Use native keyboard and accessibility behavior through existing components. Avoid focus on every rerender or every return from a message. Restore focus only when opening a fresh search or when the user selects the field. Active motion uses `theme.duration` and `theme.reduceMotion`. Secondary text/highlight combinations must maintain 4.5:1 text contrast against their actual backgrounds, including outgoing custom chat colors.
 77: 
 78: ## 5. Data, state, navigation, and compatibility contracts
 79: 
 80: ### 5.1 Matching and scope
 81: 
 82: Reuse `normalizeSearch` as the canonical delivered-message predicate foundation. Literal case-insensitive NFKC substring matching applies independently to content, resolved sender name, and live attachment-type labels. Use the same predicate for snippets, delivered counts, and scoped messages. Keep raw input for the field and username handoff. The backend's exact-username rules continue through `findUser`.
 83: 
 84: Derive eligible conversation IDs from the existing account-specific chat-list output, including ignored/archived chats. Confirm caller membership again in SQL and when opening a result. A direct scoped route must satisfy the same authorization checks. A blocked direct target must not be reopened through a People candidate supplied by a different group. Locally deleted chat messages remain excluded. An explicit person selection may reuse the established direct-chat reopening behavior after eligibility checks.
 85: 
 86: **Proposed search-local contracts**, placed in `src/search` rather than route files, include a conversation summary `{ chatId, deliveredMatchCount, latestMatch }`, a scoped page `{ matches, next }`, and request identity `{ userId, allowedChatIds, chatId?, normalizedQuery, generation }`. Preserve the existing `searchCachedMessages` callable contract unless all known callers/tests are updated together. Add optional match-source metadata for content/sender/attachment explanations. Stored names/previews are derived values rather than history identifiers.
 87: 
 88: A completed conversation summary represents one stable search generation. Aggregate all matches while retaining one summary per conversation. The existing timestamp/message-ID ordering remains the delivered cursor order. Group ordering follows latest matching timestamp, then message ID, then chat ID. Name ordering uses exact/prefix/substring class, newest existing conversation activity, normalized name, and entity ID.
 89: 
 90: Scoped search also merges relevant unsent items from `getUnsentItems`, filtered by selected chat and the authenticated sender. Use `temp_id = message_id` to deduplicate delivered echoes, preserve existing queue statuses, and subscribe to `subscribeQueueChange`. Global conversation totals remain delivered-message totals, matching existing global behavior. Scoped summaries identify queued matches separately so they do not silently inflate a delivered total. Pending target navigation must still resolve to the existing queued bubble.
 91: 
 92: Cache/queue events invalidate the affected generation, refresh counts/pages, and coalesce bursts. The current `subscribeChatPreferenceChanges` API also supplies relevant chat block/preference changes. Observe those changes for candidate scopes without altering unrelated consumers. Revalidate on focus because the screen may have been frozen or unsubscribed. Preserve scroll by entity/message key where possible. Remove stale content promptly when permission or deletion changes. Keep only one active scan and one coalesced rerun per scope. Check cancellation between batches and discard late native results. Proposed local-search deadline is 15 seconds with visible timeout and user-triggered retry. A native read already in flight may finish after cancellation, but its result cannot update UI or release another request's guard.
 93: 
 94: No migration or new runtime dependency is planned. Existing cache v7 columns/indexes and AsyncStorage cover the feature. If measured performance cannot meet SR-12 with bounded batching, document the query bottleneck and propose the smallest change before adding a schema or indexing dependency.
 95: 
 96: ### 5.2 People and creation
 97: 
 98: Add a search-local contact query anchored to allowed chat IDs and the caller's membership. Deduplicate by user ID and reuse the shared contact representation where compatible. Include contacts known only through groups. Match the canonical display name and permitted cached direct-chat nickname, while using the existing direct-chat title in its result. Keep Saved Messages discoverable as a conversation shortcut in People without creating a second self-chat.
 99: 
100: Resolve an existing eligible direct chat before using `openDirectChat`. A failed or timed-out cache/eligibility preflight produces an explicit read failure and performs no create mutation. Creation requires a successful eligible lookup that finds no existing direct chat. This caller-side guard is necessary because `openDirectChat` itself catches a cache-read failure and can proceed to creation. Preserve that helper's ordinary callers. Offline, open existing cached chats and disable creation for a person with no existing chat with explicit connection feedback. Online, use the existing creation outcome and resync handling. Hold an operation lock until resolution and ignore navigation from a previous account. Typing does not create chats or issue remote lookups.
101: 
102: Find someone by username pushes `/new-chat` with an optional raw username prefill. Add safe scalar/array parameter handling there. The existing Find action triggers the lookup and retains existing not-found, rate-limit, and network errors. Record search history after an existing conversation opens or creation successfully resolves. Support a search-origin marker in this handoff so its Back behavior returns to the originating search and returned targets receive the same blocked-target checks before opening. Preserve ordinary New Chat callers. Avoid automatic mutation retries after an ambiguous network failure. Reconcile using existing direct-chat lookup/resync before another explicit attempt.
103: 
104: ### 5.3 History
105: 
106: Proposed storage key is `dg.searchHistory:<userId>` with payload `{ version: 1, entries }`. Each entry is `{ kind: 'person' | 'chat', id: string, selectedAt: number }`, where time is epoch milliseconds. Cap entries at 20 after canonical deduplication. Selecting a known regular direct conversation, including a message result from it, records its person identity. Groups and Saved Messages record their chat identity. This collapses a direct conversation and its person into one recent item. Resolve entries against current eligibility each time they render. New accounts/absent storage begin empty. Validate malformed entries without crashing or reading another account's key. An unsupported future version is reported as unavailable history and is preserved until an explicit clear action.
107: 
108: Serialize read/modify/write mutations per account, continue the chain after failure, and use a generation guard so an earlier load cannot restore removed entries. Keep the last durable state until a write succeeds and offer explicit retry after failure. Selecting a result must still open it when recording history fails. Search-screen Clear all affects only this account's history key. Integrate a device-history cleanup helper with the existing `clearLocalData` path, which is called by sign-out. That operation removes only the feature's history-key prefix across this device, fences in-flight history mutations, and invalidates stale writers before reporting success. Its failure remains recoverable through the established sign-out error/retry path. Preserve other device preferences and saved-account behavior.
109: 
110: History reads/writes/cleanup use a 15-second logical UI deadline. A timeout is reported as an unknown durable outcome until the native promise settles. Generation guards suppress stale UI updates, but they do not cancel a physical storage write. Keep that write tracked in the serialization chain, reconcile by reading storage after it settles, and avoid overlapping mutation retries. A clear operation stays ordered after older writes and cannot report success before its own removal succeeds. Account exit invalidates older writers even when navigation has completed. If native storage never settles, show the timeout, preserve the pending operation fence, and continue independent app work. Record the blocked storage operation rather than retrying it indefinitely.
111: 
112: When history has an unsupported future version, ordinary result selections still open their destination but cannot overwrite that history payload. Show an explicit unavailable-history state with the existing clear confirmation reachable. A confirmed clear may replace it with an empty version-1 payload.
113: 
114: ### 5.4 Routes and shared callers
115: 
116: Use existing routes `/search`, `/chat/[chatId]/search`, `/chat/[chatId]`, and `/new-chat`. Proposed optional parameters are scoped `query`, chat `searchQuery` alongside existing `focusMessageId`, and username `username` plus a search-origin marker. Validate missing/array parameters and account scope. Raw queries are navigation state, not analytics or diagnostic output.
117: 
118: Use a push/return flow that retains the scoped screen. Verify actual Expo Router 57 stack behavior when the same chat is already underneath search. Existing chat and pinned routes also use `focusMessageId`; optional search metadata must preserve their established behavior. Missing or deleted search targets get explicit feedback without changing non-search helper callers' expectations.
119: 
120: Extend SearchField with a backward-compatible controlled/uncontrolled API. Render and clear from one current value. Preserve immediate clearing, 150 ms default callback debounce for forward-search callers, and timer cleanup. Global/scoped inputs update immediately while the query layer controls debounce. Ensure submission uses current text even with a pending timer.
121: 
122: Pass an optional highlight query through MessageBubble into MessageText. Preserve Markdown AST structure, code/font styles, URL handling, image suppression, and native text selection/actions. Unicode highlight ranges must map normalized matches to original display text without changing the saved or displayed message. A query spanning multiple rendered Markdown leaves requires contiguous rendered-text range handling. For matches found only in hidden Markdown syntax or a link destination, retain the focused-row highlight and explain the matched raw content in the search snippet rather than inventing a visible term highlight.
123: 
124: ## 6. Execution phases and measurable work items
125: 
126: These phases are specific to search and do not inherit the numbering or incomplete release gates of the older Android plan. Items within each phase have equal weight. An item earns credit only after its stated work and applicable acceptance assertions pass. A recorded failed, blocked, or unrun required assertion does not earn completion credit. Shared execution rules in Section 8 apply throughout. Source baselines and recoverable snapshots gate code changes. Unavailable runtime prerequisites may leave their Phase 0 items incomplete while Phases 1–3 proceed on satisfied source/test dependencies. Report that distinction explicitly and retain the unfinished items in the denominator. For P0 baseline capture, completing the work means capturing and classifying the actual baseline result, including a pre-existing failure, rather than requiring the unchanged application to pass every repository check before coding can start.
127: 
128: Verification IDs group related assertions across phases. Their ownership below defines which assertions are due at each gate. Listing a group in a work item does not claim the whole group has passed before later integration exists.
129: 
130: | Phase | Assertions owned by its items and gate |
131: |---|---|
132: | Phase 0 | V00 source/snapshot preparation and V10 existing-test/toolchain baseline capture. Do not attempt newly proposed test paths as baseline files. P0.2/P0.3 runtime discovery or observations may remain pending without blocking independently satisfied code prerequisites. |
133: | Phase 1 | V01 query/count/cursor assertions, V02 field contract, V03 people adapter/ranking, V04 scoped-data/pending merge, V07 request lifecycle, and V08 pure highlight-range helpers. Screen creation/opening and Markdown-renderer integration are due later. |
134: | Phase 2 | V02 global screen, V03 person opening/username handoff, V06 history/cleanup, V07 global/history state, V05 global route-parameter handoff, and V09 affected field/New Chat/cleanup callers. |
135: | Phase 3 | V04 scoped UI/target selection, V05 full navigation/state retention, V07 scoped/context invalidation, V08 Markdown-renderer highlights, and V09 historical/pinned/account/calling-consumer preservation. |
136: | Phase 4 | Final affected V00–V10 checks, actual V11 platform/performance observation, cleanup, and consolidated SR acceptance. Earlier assertion evidence can be reused when its source/environment identities remain applicable. |
